This past weekend I played a few games as a Ranger, a first for me.  In
one of them I found Cleaver just sitting around by itself on one of the
upper dungeon levels.  Not what I would really have requested, but
whatever, I made it my primary melee weapon, and bumped up my skill with
it when I was able to.

Later I'm coming back up out of Sokoban after getting its bag of
holding, and a swarm of killer bees comes at me down the hallway on the
bottom (first) level.  I think I used a wand and/or arrows to take most
of them out, but one lucky guy closed to melee.  "No problem," I
thought, "have a taste of Cleaver!"  I began swinging away...and missing
every time.  Whiff, whiff, whiff.  Meanwhile the bee is stinging the
crap out of me; as I recall, he took me from HPs in the 50s down to
about 7.  I'm flabbergasted by this turn of events, but it's been a
while since I prayed, so I figured I should do that.  Sting, sting,
DYWYPI?

I'm in utter shock, numbly scrolling through the game-ending messages,
and I notice that it says "You were extremely unlucky."  No kidding, but
why?  I consult the Wiki page on Luck, and soon one line jumped out at
me: "Jumping in Sokoban."  Ohhhhhh, right.

Early in the game I'd found a spellbook, and after curse-testing it, I
hazarded a read and it turned out to be jumping.  After that, whenever I
thought of it and noticed that my power was maxed out, I'd jump around
until I'd exhausted my power to build up the skill.  And I'd done that
in Sokoban just before coming to the level with the bees.

Honestly, it's never even occurred to me to use jumping to cheat in
Sokoban.  Ahh, well.  Yet another object lesson to drill that little bit
of Nethack lore into my brain really deep.


